1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,510
Internet Explorer 6 was dead.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,090
At least, Microsoft said it was.

3
00:00:05,938 --> 00:00:08,948
The company had spent years
telling people to upgrade.

4
00:00:09,838 --> 00:00:13,148
Firefox was better. Chrome was better.

5
00:00:13,878 --> 00:00:16,028
Internet Explorer 7 was better.

6
00:00:16,778 --> 00:00:18,828
Internet Explorer 8 was better.

7
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:22,320
There was a countdown on
Microsoft's own website.

8
00:00:22,610 --> 00:00:24,420
Percent by percent.

9
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:26,300
Country by country.

10
00:00:27,091 --> 00:00:32,781
And yet, in 2010, millions of computers
were still running Internet Explorer 6.

11
00:00:33,630 --> 00:00:35,710
Not because their owners liked it.

12
00:00:35,790 --> 00:00:38,690
Not because Microsoft wanted them to.

13
00:00:38,770 --> 00:00:41,740
Because something they
needed depended on it.

14
00:00:42,455 --> 00:00:47,065
And that is how a browser stops being
software. It becomes infrastructure.

15
00:00:48,249 --> 00:00:50,679
The strange thing is, Microsoft knew.

16
00:00:50,829 --> 00:00:53,939
They were trying to kill
Internet Explorer 6.

17
00:00:54,489 --> 00:00:58,539
And at the same time, they were selling
companies a way to keep it alive.

18
00:00:59,315 --> 00:01:03,865
This is part three. Part one was how
one browser became that powerful.

19
00:01:04,735 --> 00:01:07,645
Part two was what it did to
the people building for it.

20
00:01:08,075 --> 00:01:09,705
Both are linked below.

21
00:01:10,553 --> 00:01:12,383
This one is the harder question.

22
00:01:13,193 --> 00:01:16,343
What happens when obsolete
software becomes infrastructure?

23
00:01:19,343 --> 00:01:21,653
Start in January, 2010.

24
00:01:22,843 --> 00:01:28,753
Internet Explorer 6 is nine years
old. Firefox is six. Chrome is two.

25
00:01:29,323 --> 00:01:32,913
And more than one browser in ten
is still Internet Explorer 6.

26
00:01:33,633 --> 00:01:37,243
Then Google discovers that
somebody is inside its own network.

27
00:01:38,124 --> 00:01:39,344
Not a defaced website.

28
00:01:39,424 --> 00:01:40,944
Source code.

29
00:01:41,024 --> 00:01:42,584
Employee accounts.

30
00:01:42,664 --> 00:01:46,794
The Gmail accounts of Chinese
human rights activists.

31
00:01:47,805 --> 00:01:51,275
And at least twenty other companies
had been opened the same way.

32
00:01:51,997 --> 00:01:54,397
The way in was a flaw in
Internet Explorer that

33
00:01:54,477 --> 00:01:57,747
nobody had fixed, because
nobody knew it was there.

34
00:01:58,757 --> 00:02:01,587
The attack used it against
Internet Explorer 6.

35
00:02:02,441 --> 00:02:07,091
The researchers who took it apart
gave the operation a name. Aurora.

36
00:02:07,941 --> 00:02:09,401
And Microsoft broke its own monthly

37
00:02:09,481 --> 00:02:12,391
schedule to ship the
fix, three weeks early.

38
00:02:13,165 --> 00:02:16,015
And then something happened
that had not happened before.

39
00:02:17,005 --> 00:02:19,875
Governments told their citizens
to stop using a browser.

40
00:02:20,879 --> 00:02:26,249
Germany's federal security office said
use something else. France said the same.

41
00:02:27,239 --> 00:02:29,369
Firefox. Chrome. Anything but this.

42
00:02:30,089 --> 00:02:32,379
In Germany, people
downloaded three hundred

43
00:02:32,459 --> 00:02:35,379
thousand copies of Firefox
over a single weekend.

44
00:02:36,265 --> 00:02:38,755
A few weeks later, Google stopped waiting.

45
00:02:39,705 --> 00:02:42,385
From the first of March,
Google Docs and Google Sites

46
00:02:42,465 --> 00:02:45,755
would no longer work properly
in Internet Explorer 6.

47
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:50,150
So by the spring of 2010,
nobody was arguing anymore.

48
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:53,150
The browser was a security problem.

49
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:55,210
Two governments said so.

50
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:57,150
Google said so.

51
00:02:57,798 --> 00:02:59,328
And it still didn't go away.

52
00:03:00,058 --> 00:03:01,868
That is the part worth explaining.

53
00:03:04,858 --> 00:03:07,268
The usual explanation is Windows XP.

54
00:03:08,258 --> 00:03:12,568
Old operating system, old browser,
nobody upgraded, end of story.

55
00:03:13,298 --> 00:03:14,928
That explanation is wrong.

56
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:19,340
Internet Explorer 7 came out in 2006.

57
00:03:19,610 --> 00:03:21,480
It ran on Windows XP.

58
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:25,710
Internet Explorer 8 came out in 2009.

59
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,550
It ran on Windows XP too.

60
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:30,490
That is where it stopped.

61
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:36,810
Internet Explorer 9, in 2011,
would not run on Windows XP at all.

62
00:03:37,499 --> 00:03:40,339
Microsoft even pushed
Internet Explorer 7 out

63
00:03:40,419 --> 00:03:43,289
through Windows Update,
as a critical update.

64
00:03:43,879 --> 00:03:47,109
If you had automatic
updates on, it came to you.

65
00:03:47,773 --> 00:03:53,343
So from 2006 onwards, a machine running
Windows XP could have a newer browser.

66
00:03:54,453 --> 00:03:57,443
That is four years before
anybody had heard of Aurora.

67
00:03:58,197 --> 00:04:00,977
Which means that every machine
still on Internet Explorer

68
00:04:01,057 --> 00:04:05,987
6 in 2010 was not stuck there
by its operating system.

69
00:04:06,783 --> 00:04:09,033
The operating system wasn't the reason.

70
00:04:09,263 --> 00:04:10,653
The application was.

71
00:04:13,709 --> 00:04:16,129
Somewhere inside that
company there was a piece of

72
00:04:16,209 --> 00:04:19,759
software that only worked
in Internet Explorer 6.

73
00:04:20,567 --> 00:04:22,917
Not a website. An application.

74
00:04:24,027 --> 00:04:26,847
It ran on ActiveX, so it
could reach the file system,

75
00:04:26,927 --> 00:04:29,737
a printer, a scanner, a
database, a card reader.

76
00:04:30,502 --> 00:04:35,852
It had been written when Internet Explorer
6 was the only target anybody cared about.

77
00:04:36,486 --> 00:04:38,856
And it worked. That is the awkward part.

78
00:04:39,626 --> 00:04:42,596
It worked every morning, and
people ran their day on it.

79
00:04:43,304 --> 00:04:46,114
Change the browser, and you
are not upgrading software.

80
00:04:47,004 --> 00:04:48,744
You are betting that
something nobody fully

81
00:04:48,824 --> 00:04:51,354
understands still behaves the same way.

82
00:04:53,734 --> 00:04:56,524
Microsoft understood
that better than anybody.

83
00:04:57,457 --> 00:04:59,937
Because when it shipped
Internet Explorer 7 through

84
00:05:00,017 --> 00:05:03,167
Windows Update, its
biggest customers panicked.

85
00:05:04,059 --> 00:05:07,849
Ten thousand machines.
One automatic update.

86
00:05:07,959 --> 00:05:12,869
Every internal application that had
ever assumed Internet Explorer 6.

87
00:05:13,695 --> 00:05:16,125
So Microsoft did something remarkable.

88
00:05:16,995 --> 00:05:19,275
It published a tool
whose only purpose was to

89
00:05:19,355 --> 00:05:21,905
stop its own new browser
from being installed.

90
00:05:22,796 --> 00:05:24,586
It was called the Blocker Toolkit.

91
00:05:25,096 --> 00:05:27,816
You ran it, and it wrote a
setting that told Windows

92
00:05:27,896 --> 00:05:31,306
Update never to offer
Internet Explorer 7 again.

93
00:05:32,067 --> 00:05:33,777
There was no expiry date.

94
00:05:34,745 --> 00:05:39,155
Then Internet Explorer 8 came out, and
Microsoft published a blocker for that.

95
00:05:39,945 --> 00:05:43,155
Then one for nine. Then one for eleven.

96
00:05:43,954 --> 00:05:45,524
That is worth saying twice.

97
00:05:46,494 --> 00:05:50,244
The company that wanted you to upgrade
also shipped the switch for not upgrading.

98
00:05:51,054 --> 00:05:52,254
Four times.

99
00:05:53,014 --> 00:05:55,144
And it wasn't an accident. It was demand.

100
00:05:56,154 --> 00:05:58,964
Microsoft was not fighting
its customers' inertia.

101
00:05:59,314 --> 00:06:01,124
It was supplying it.

102
00:06:01,839 --> 00:06:04,249
But blocking an update only buys time.

103
00:06:05,099 --> 00:06:09,449
Sooner or later the machine itself
dies, and somebody has to buy a new one.

104
00:06:12,039 --> 00:06:15,969
And after 2009, a new
machine meant Windows 7.

105
00:06:16,679 --> 00:06:23,149
Windows 7 would not run Internet Explorer
6. Not with a blocker. Not with a setting.

106
00:06:23,799 --> 00:06:25,309
The browser was simply gone.

107
00:06:26,125 --> 00:06:27,975
So the company had a choice.

108
00:06:28,665 --> 00:06:33,015
Stay on an operating system that Microsoft
would stop patching in a few years.

109
00:06:33,685 --> 00:06:35,095
Or replace the application.

110
00:06:36,124 --> 00:06:38,254
Microsoft offered a third option.

111
00:06:39,324 --> 00:06:43,114
And this is the part that is hard to
believe until you see the product page.

112
00:06:44,030 --> 00:06:47,780
It was called Microsoft
Enterprise Desktop Virtualization.

113
00:06:48,410 --> 00:06:51,400
Large companies bought it
as part of a subscription.

114
00:06:52,405 --> 00:06:56,665
What it did was run a complete copy
of Windows XP inside Windows 7.

115
00:06:56,745 --> 00:06:58,585
Invisibly.

116
00:06:58,665 --> 00:07:00,055
In the background.

117
00:07:01,103 --> 00:07:05,673
And inside that hidden Windows
XP, it ran Internet Explorer 6.

118
00:07:06,511 --> 00:07:08,361
The second version went further.

119
00:07:09,251 --> 00:07:11,421
An administrator gave
it a list of addresses.

120
00:07:12,391 --> 00:07:16,801
Anything on that list opened in the
old browser, inside the hidden machine.

121
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:20,490
The employee clicked a
link and a window appeared.

122
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:22,920
They never knew they had just started an

123
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,730
entire operating system
to look at one page.

124
00:07:26,634 --> 00:07:29,744
So look at what Microsoft
was doing in the same year.

125
00:07:30,094 --> 00:07:33,584
Telling the world that
Internet Explorer 6 had to go.

126
00:07:34,074 --> 00:07:36,674
And selling its largest
customers a product

127
00:07:36,754 --> 00:07:39,164
whose whole purpose
was keeping it running.

128
00:07:39,961 --> 00:07:42,851
Microsoft was killing Internet Explorer 6.

129
00:07:43,061 --> 00:07:46,731
And Microsoft was also selling
you a way to keep it alive.

130
00:07:49,531 --> 00:07:54,621
And that isn't hypocrisy. That is what
a dependency looks like from the inside.

131
00:07:55,791 --> 00:07:59,001
Even the company that made the
thing can't simply take it away.

132
00:07:59,849 --> 00:08:01,619
It didn't stop there, either.

133
00:08:02,389 --> 00:08:05,889
A few years later, Internet Explorer
11 shipped with compatibility

134
00:08:05,969 --> 00:08:11,039
modes for older sites, one of them
behaving like Internet Explorer 8.

135
00:08:11,809 --> 00:08:13,789
It was there for the old
internal applications

136
00:08:13,869 --> 00:08:17,059
and the ActiveX controls inside them.

137
00:08:18,209 --> 00:08:22,579
Microsoft's own documentation said many
of those would simply keep working.

138
00:08:23,341 --> 00:08:25,991
The old browser was no
longer a different machine.

139
00:08:26,841 --> 00:08:28,791
It was a setting inside the new one.

140
00:08:31,231 --> 00:08:35,741
Up to here this sounds like a story
about companies. Bad software.

141
00:08:36,431 --> 00:08:38,121
Cautious managers. Budgets.

142
00:08:39,011 --> 00:08:41,881
In South Korea it was a
story about a country.

143
00:08:42,734 --> 00:08:45,634
In the late nineties,
Korean regulations required

144
00:08:45,714 --> 00:08:48,784
online payments to use a
particular kind of encryption.

145
00:08:50,094 --> 00:08:51,924
No browser supported it.

146
00:08:52,581 --> 00:08:54,911
The only way to add it was ActiveX.

147
00:08:55,761 --> 00:08:59,151
And the only browser with
ActiveX was Internet Explorer.

148
00:09:00,039 --> 00:09:02,289
So banking ran on ActiveX.

149
00:09:02,799 --> 00:09:05,179
Government sites ran on ActiveX.

150
00:09:05,639 --> 00:09:07,539
Shopping ran on ActiveX.

151
00:09:07,919 --> 00:09:10,659
Anything involving money
or identity arrived

152
00:09:10,739 --> 00:09:13,189
with a plug-in you had to install first.

153
00:09:13,987 --> 00:09:17,797
A whole country's online life now
depended on one company's browser.

154
00:09:18,247 --> 00:09:21,537
Not by accident. By regulation.

155
00:09:22,684 --> 00:09:25,494
The requirement was lifted in 2014.

156
00:09:26,244 --> 00:09:28,654
The websites kept the plug-ins anyway.

157
00:09:29,481 --> 00:09:32,271
That is the difference between a
bad decision and infrastructure.

158
00:09:33,381 --> 00:09:35,131
A bad decision can be reversed.

159
00:09:36,361 --> 00:09:37,931
Infrastructure has to be replaced.

160
00:09:40,641 --> 00:09:42,651
And you could see the same thing on a map.

161
00:09:43,681 --> 00:09:47,561
In January 2012, Internet
Explorer 6 finally

162
00:09:47,641 --> 00:09:50,351
fell below one percent
in the United States.

163
00:09:51,192 --> 00:09:53,612
In China, that same winter, a quarter of

164
00:09:53,692 --> 00:09:56,442
all browsers were still
Internet Explorer 6.

165
00:09:57,392 --> 00:09:59,522
In the spring before, it had been a third.

166
00:10:00,378 --> 00:10:03,768
Same web. Same browser. Same winter.

167
00:10:04,638 --> 00:10:07,428
Because the question was
never which browser is better.

168
00:10:08,238 --> 00:10:10,888
It was what somebody had
already built on top of it.

169
00:10:11,595 --> 00:10:14,205
And some of what had been
built on it was not small.

170
00:10:15,395 --> 00:10:19,675
In September 2013, eighty-five
percent of the computers in

171
00:10:19,755 --> 00:10:23,485
Britain's national health service
were still running Windows XP.

172
00:10:24,429 --> 00:10:27,211
Out of roughly eight hundred thousand.

173
00:10:27,661 --> 00:10:31,831
Support for Windows XP was
going to end in April 2014.

174
00:10:33,175 --> 00:10:35,745
So the British government
signed a deal with Microsoft.

175
00:10:36,735 --> 00:10:39,835
Five and a half million
pounds for twelve more months

176
00:10:39,915 --> 00:10:43,385
of security updates across
the whole public sector.

177
00:10:44,196 --> 00:10:45,846
Not to upgrade anything.

178
00:10:45,996 --> 00:10:48,646
To buy one more year of not upgrading.

179
00:10:49,627 --> 00:10:53,917
That is what a dependency costs
once somebody puts a number on it.

180
00:10:54,609 --> 00:10:56,594
And the reason that
year was worth five and

181
00:10:56,674 --> 00:10:59,259
a half million pounds
is the same everywhere.

182
00:11:00,081 --> 00:11:05,251
Before you can replace the old thing, you
have to find out what it does. All of it.

183
00:11:05,901 --> 00:11:09,231
Including the parts nobody
wrote down and nobody remembers.

184
00:11:10,122 --> 00:11:11,892
You don't just inherit the software.

185
00:11:12,722 --> 00:11:16,032
You inherit every assumption
anybody ever made about it.

186
00:11:16,802 --> 00:11:19,692
And after ten years nobody
knows what all of them are.

187
00:11:22,312 --> 00:11:25,582
So how did it actually
end? Not with a deadline.

188
00:11:26,634 --> 00:11:31,504
In December 2011, Microsoft
announced that it would stop asking.

189
00:11:32,319 --> 00:11:34,819
From then on, Windows would
upgrade Internet Explorer

190
00:11:34,899 --> 00:11:38,469
by itself, to the newest
version the machine could run.

191
00:11:39,619 --> 00:11:41,509
On Windows XP, that was eight.

192
00:11:42,316 --> 00:11:43,806
You could still say no.

193
00:11:44,596 --> 00:11:48,066
Most people didn't, because most
people never knew there was a question.

194
00:11:48,878 --> 00:11:53,648
That winter, Internet Explorer 6 went
under one percent in the United States.

195
00:11:54,499 --> 00:12:00,089
Support for Windows XP ended in
April 2014, exactly as promised.

196
00:12:00,869 --> 00:12:03,469
Two years after the
browser had already lost.

197
00:12:04,304 --> 00:12:06,154
And even that line didn't hold.

198
00:12:07,024 --> 00:12:09,684
Three weeks later a serious
flaw turned up in Internet

199
00:12:09,764 --> 00:12:13,874
Explorer, and Microsoft patched
it on Windows XP anyway.

200
00:12:14,663 --> 00:12:17,023
Internet Explorer 6 was
still getting security

201
00:12:17,103 --> 00:12:21,253
updates somewhere in the
world until January 2016.

202
00:12:21,583 --> 00:12:23,773
Fourteen years after it shipped.

203
00:12:24,615 --> 00:12:29,005
Internet Explorer itself was
finally switched off in June 2022.

204
00:12:30,450 --> 00:12:34,050
And Microsoft Edge still carries
a mode that draws pages the way

205
00:12:34,130 --> 00:12:38,280
Internet Explorer did, because
too many companies still need it.

206
00:12:39,110 --> 00:12:44,180
Microsoft has promised to keep that
mode working until at least 2029.

207
00:12:45,304 --> 00:12:47,274
Twenty-eight years after
the browser shipped.

208
00:12:47,424 --> 00:12:50,434
Nineteen years after it was declared dead.

209
00:12:53,284 --> 00:12:56,394
So that is the answer to the
question we started with.

210
00:12:57,135 --> 00:12:58,825
A browser becomes a platform.

211
00:12:59,375 --> 00:13:01,445
A platform becomes a dependency.

212
00:13:02,335 --> 00:13:04,325
A dependency becomes infrastructure.

213
00:13:05,375 --> 00:13:07,625
Infrastructure becomes technical debt.

214
00:13:08,055 --> 00:13:10,765
And technical debt becomes somebody's job.

215
00:13:13,555 --> 00:13:16,405
Usually not the job of
the person who built it.

216
00:13:17,085 --> 00:13:19,435
And once enough things
depend on something,

217
00:13:19,525 --> 00:13:22,295
being better is no longer
enough to replace it.

218
00:13:22,825 --> 00:13:25,155
Everybody else has to move as well.

219
00:13:25,887 --> 00:13:28,297
Which is the strangest
thing about technology.

220
00:13:29,187 --> 00:13:32,137
Sometimes the thing that wins
isn't the thing that survives.

221
00:13:33,147 --> 00:13:36,117
Sometimes it becomes the
thing everyone has to survive.

222
00:13:39,627 --> 00:13:43,737
Internet Explorer 6 didn't die
when Microsoft stopped making it.

223
00:13:44,353 --> 00:13:47,423
It died when enough of the
world stopped depending on it.

224
00:13:48,118 --> 00:13:49,888
And that took a very long time.
